#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3601edf3-7bc3-5e47-9192-e196ae0e963b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375900} {:answer/id #uuid "6e0da404-720d-5418-b847-1a43cc313cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375901} {:answer/id #uuid "5b6e3c0a-30bc-59bd-bd90-15f5376494ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375902} {:answer/id #uuid "e76f095c-d497-5e72-9442-303c5e7a57be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375903} {:answer/id #uuid "20e3bf36-2efb-5912-9b29-2abb28d142a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20e3bf36-2efb-5912-9b29-2abb28d142a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375904} :answer-field/id #uuid "937c088a-c3b6-5cb8-aaa4-2226b64c1b21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d189a361-c4b6-51b0-8803-3d2da2828666" :question/math-academy-id "q-110761" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $A={⟨2t,2+t⟩\\,:\\,t\\in R}$\n- $B={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x-y=0}$\n- $C={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=1}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. If the origin belongs to this subset, we would have, for some $t$:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 2+t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives us\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t=0 \\\\ 2+t=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the system has no solution. So, $0$ is not in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace.  It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x-y=0$ since $0-0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $y=1$ since $0\\ne 1.$ So, $0$ is not in $C.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a8dbbab-2dea-5fff-86f0-3411f5405ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15b102c6-52d8-5b4b-b89e-5d649a149a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375907} {:answer/id #uuid "7b09d73f-7b77-5e95-8991-6ca5a8971844" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375908} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c23ad4-a08f-5509-8889-af165b8c63cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375909} {:answer/id #uuid "3f421a8d-15cc-5f11-a51f-88d9ddfdee23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375910} {:answer/id #uuid "68c0ce32-21f4-5e2b-a162-ea78eefd6a8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68c0ce32-21f4-5e2b-a162-ea78eefd6a8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375911} :answer-field/id #uuid "648718ce-02cf-59fe-b2ff-5e8c18a6da25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1cf36aea-1a3d-5f57-9970-f75b927a4791" :question/math-academy-id "q-158100" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/assets/003b4f77a413444989bcec8b9682465419a58ded55ed95cefb4f43c122bdc3d3.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af5c3b1d-8dac-5923-80f7-00e4eca77470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375914} {:answer/id #uuid "6d9bb4c9-850c-5b6b-8168-665b8168e88e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375915} {:answer/id #uuid "acf6188f-68a6-59e7-b380-812cad7e20af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375916} {:answer/id #uuid "037018be-1f69-578f-881d-2975ecfdb3bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375917} {:answer/id #uuid "feffaf29-adc3-5373-ae58-c9ef05a5b1df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af5c3b1d-8dac-5923-80f7-00e4eca77470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375914} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e3acf2d-2323-55af-8e64-c9950a843dff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01166beb-7893-5365-92e5-cedbd35f370b" :question/math-academy-id "q-34327" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $A={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0}$\n- $B={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x=2y}$\n- $C={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x-y=0}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It actually represents the whole space ${R}^{2}$, since the coordinates of any vector $⟨x,y⟩$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x=2y$ since $0=2\\cdot 0.$ So, $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It also represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x-y=0$ since $0-0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a8dbbab-2dea-5fff-86f0-3411f5405ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "840db760-7932-509c-b2c2-e198c87fb021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191665} {:answer/id #uuid "9f80b055-ec54-5836-9a30-949505229b5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191666} {:answer/id #uuid "b3594b70-bbb7-5855-b13c-ffa79706ca36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191667} {:answer/id #uuid "b983476c-4c22-5606-bfda-86e94c7cfe80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191668} {:answer/id #uuid "5b6c42ee-deaa-562d-a866-abf5d3b13a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b983476c-4c22-5606-bfda-86e94c7cfe80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191668} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d4fabca-b315-5e3e-80c6-ac46972ed470" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99d3ff8e-37f2-5365-be21-7d8861823943" :question/math-academy-id "q-35595" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070328/assets/df3b62554600601401d14d3b6758a3c350cf8cddc3788e79a3ba456a5a19c60a.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47b35ff3-1d83-5ca1-bcc4-f15101f4d50b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375921} {:answer/id #uuid "7b2e92a6-3564-5527-a3ad-41c7c6f38ab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375922} {:answer/id #uuid "37b7346d-c46a-50b1-8651-20de4ac00c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375923} {:answer/id #uuid "7c8216c6-9bed-5449-b02a-cd20fd7f1fee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375924} {:answer/id #uuid "85bd939b-cea6-5d54-89c8-be44f4c1141f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85bd939b-cea6-5d54-89c8-be44f4c1141f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375925} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc864d67-6e0c-5113-a4a0-7632b4715c9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0f2b0a81-556c-5bc3-bde3-5b6da6eef31a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74255" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $A={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}<\\frac{1}{2}}$\n- $B={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:5x-y-z=0}$\n- $C={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:5-x-z=0}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace. Indeed, the vector $u=⟨\\frac{1}{4},0,0⟩$ is in $A$, since\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}<\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nbut, $4u=⟨1,0,0⟩$ is not in $B$, since \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}>\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $5x-y-z=0$ since $5\\cdot 0-0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $5-x-z=0$ since $5-0-0\\ne 0.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
